        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains under acute operational stress, with the Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure composite sitting in the extreme band, flat over the past 24 hours, driven primarily by physical transit deviation. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports covering both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 418 vessels in the most recent 24 hours. For context on through-strait traffic, PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-08-02, recorded exactly 2 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day, though that figure carries a multi-day lag as PortWatch publishes weekly on Tuesdays. The Escalation Forecast composite sits in the elevated band and is easing over the horizon mix measured, though forecasters flag low confidence due to thin contract volume, with Polymarket escalation contracts the top contributor; that easing reading diverges from the still-extreme state composite and should not be read as a clearance signal. Brent crude stood at $83.55, down 0.54% over the preceding 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC; futures markets are currently closed, making that a carried-forward pre-close figure rather than a live trading signal. On the diplomatic track, an IRGC spokesperson stated via Iranian state media, treat as a claim, that any reopening of the strait is governed by Iran's own conditions and has no connection to Iran-Oman talks. At least 70 ships remain trapped in the Persian Gulf, per USNI News. gCaptain reports ADNOC confirmed three vessel attacks this week.",
